        "id": "unit_9_q_1",
        "rowNumber": 2,
        "sNo": 1,
        "question": "The concept of totipotency of plant cells, forming the basis of tissue culture, was first proposed by:",
        "optionA": "Gottlieb Haberlandt",
        "optionB": "Skoog and Miller",
        "optionC": "Murashige and Skoog",
        "optionD": "F. C. Steward",
        "correctOption": "A",
        "analysisA": "Correct. Haberlandt (1902) proposed that any living plant cell can regenerate a whole plant.",
        "analysisB": "Incorrect. Skoog and Miller worked out the auxin-cytokinin balance for organogenesis.",
        "analysisC": "Incorrect. Murashige and Skoog developed the widely used MS medium (1962).",
        "analysisD": "Incorrect. Steward demonstrated totipotency experimentally using carrot cell cultures (1958).",
        "referenceNote": "Syllabus reference: Unit-9 > Plant Tissue Culture and Organogenesis: totipotency\nTotipotency is the ability of a differentiated cell to dedifferentiate, divide and regenerate a complete plant.",
